WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:05.030 align:start position:0%
 
hi everyone  welcome to our talk on pythons in python  wildlife trade data analysis using  python  my name is andevan song and together  with my collaborator literal  we are excited to talk about wildlife  trade data analysis  using python  the global wildlife trade network is a  beast of a trade network  it is a multi-billion dollar industry  where millions of animals and plants are  traded within  and between countries for food medicine  pet trade gardens zoos and more  along with climate change deforestation  and fire  the wildlife trade is among the top  contributors to the global decline of  species  not only that the wildlife trade  facilitates the spread of disease around  the world which is bad for wildlife  livestock and humans the majority of  emerging  infectious diseases that are of concern  to humans  come from animals finally the wildlife  trade facilitates a species  invasion where non-native animals are  introduced into ecosystems which are  unable to adjust  to solve any of the problems surrounding  the wildlife trade  we need to have data about the trade so  where can we get information online  about the global wildlife trade  two great databases exist online  the fao or the food and agricultural  organization of the un  has cost scale data about trade between  countries  for example it tells you how many  non-food live animals are traded  but not the species traded on the other  hand  cities which is the convention of  international trade  in endangered species of wild flora and  fauna  has fine scale data which specifies  which species is being traded  it has over four shear data set with  more than 20 million  shipment records these can be downloaded  from the online database in  csv format to give you an example of  what a row of data look like  let's examine the screen capture in the  year 2013  a shipment of white-tailed eagles  scientific name  helioetis albacilla was traded to latvia  from the ukraine  along with this information we have  higher taxonomic information such  such as class aids which means it's a  bird  and falconiforms which tells us that  this is a group with falcons and related  birds  however the siteis database is a really  large database where one has to query  and download  multiple csvs to access the data and  many rows of data are missing crucial  information  in r there's an excellent existing  package called citiesdb which supports  the analysis of society's trade shipment  level data  and we want to create a similar package  in python  easy and reproducible access to this  database is useful for people who work  in  industry economics public health and  species conservation and these are  our target audience this past this  package is also useful for students for  example we recently conducted a course  at the university of rhode island for  network analysis of the wildlife trade  this package can also be useful for  researchers with more quantitative and  programming skills  as we provide streamlined access to  download this database  so here's what we had so far for our  package cites  i will describe the two main components  of this package first  data compilation and second data access  and analysis of the downloaded data  here is a flow chart representing the  steps that we take to produce a single  clean  data frame from online databases  pipes ids downloads the entire database  from the siteis trade database  at this web address in its raw phone  it consists of many cse files with  missing data  all these csv files are then merged into  a single data frame which then draws  from another database called  integrated taxonomic information systems  this allows us to computationally fill  in any blanks the data has for taxonomic  information  and correct any errors this also allows  first to use the most  updated taxonomic information for a  species as these  do change over time this complete data  frame with many of the blanks filled in  can then be saved as a csv file  so let's look at a realistic application  of this package  if you are a practitioner working in the  wildlife trade it is likely that you  would like to subset the data  by a certain characteristic for example  you might only be interested in trade  between say u.s and china  or you might be interested in only the  year 2008.  our package allows for easy filtration  of the data by your criteria of choice  in this example we will subset all data  rows with the genus  python which we all know are large  snakes that constrict their prey  specifically i want to extract all the  data about pythons imported into the usa  between  1996 and 2017.  as you can see in the code above  using my new subset of data i can then  go on to ask questions about the data  using my metric or statistic of choice  for example how many shipments are there  of different species of pythons  in the first chart in the bar chart you  can see how many shipments of eight  different species of pythons there are  in our data set  you could also plot a time series and  ask how has the shipment of these eight  different species  changed over the years from 1996 to  present  with just a few lines of codes one can  extract data from a huge database  subset and create descriptive statistics  with informative graphs  pi side uses work in progress as we all  are  we have four immediate goals first is  the improved handling of large  multi-gigabyte data sets with out of  core data frames  we also are designing an easy interface  for non-python users to be able to  subset and visualize  data we hope to incorporate network  analytic and visualization tools  which lend themselves very nicely to  understand this  sort of dyadic and relational data  finally we are interested to solicit  feedback from students and peers  and practitioners for additional useful  features for this package  that's all we have time for thank you  for listening and here is the link to  our piscis package on github  we invite you to peruse and collaborate  and look us up on social media as well  thank you very much

